  "narrative": {
    "intro": "South Dakota has received $780M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 3,457 awards with an average award size of $226K. Obligations fell 3.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $338M in obligations, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $135M and the Department of the Interior with $115M. CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC is the top vendor with $86.6M, followed by KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC with $70.9M and STERLING COMPUTERS CORPORATION with $50.1M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $211M, with Computer Facilities Management Services next at $71.2M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did FY2026 federal contract obligations compare with prior years?",
        "body": "FY2026 to date obligations total $780M, which follows FY25 obligations of $804M and FY24 obligations of $1.3B. Obligations fell 3.0% year over year. The fiscal series shows earlier totals of $624M in FY21, $1.2B in FY22, and $1B in FY23."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which buyers, vendors, and industries dominate federal spending in South Dakota?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $338M, the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $135M, and the Department of the Interior obligated $115M. CONTI FEDERAL SERVICES, LLC holds $86.6M in obligations, KBR WYLE SERVICES, LLC holds $70.9M, and STERLING COMPUTERS CORPORATION holds $50.1M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads industries with $211M, and Computer Facilities Management Services follows with $71.2M."
      }
    ]
  },
